{"authored" [{"question" "q-49309" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/assets/af84278943fcf2d9c4e3b99c115a4d82672fe50f2bfed8be7be84648985f80df.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. It consists of two lines and the arc of a semi-circle of radius $2.$ Given $g(t)={\\int }_{-3}^{t}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ determine the value of $g(3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "e64186c4f6d99028dc5d92ef4aab7f082fa435dfa296219d1ca4c5359c169245" "basis" 1148 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-49309" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=3,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(3)={\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/assets/4d1f408db7fcb99092f7e61afc4c0a25501d3349790e54983f481408c58f9951.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x\\in [-3,1],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a semi-circle and a rectangle below it. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{1}.$\n\n- $x\\in [1,3],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a trapezoid. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(3)={\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x\\in [-3,1],$ we compute the area ${A}_{1}$ of the semi-circle and the rectangle below it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2}+bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}+4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\pi +4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x\\in [1,3],$ we compute the area ${A}_{2}$ of the trapezoid as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}h(a+b) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot (1+3) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(3) & ={\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=(2\\pi +4)+4=2\\pi +8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "e64186c4f6d99028dc5d92ef4aab7f082fa435dfa296219d1ca4c5359c169245" "basis" 1148 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-49309" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "da3ddabd6e1b99db68538a49fbeacfe91dd1f81d24b1020a197b6e80597ac2ba" "basis" 1148 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-49309" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "e64186c4f6d99028dc5d92ef4aab7f082fa435dfa296219d1ca4c5359c169245" "basis" 1148 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-49309" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\pi +16"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "e64186c4f6d99028dc5d92ef4aab7f082fa435dfa296219d1ca4c5359c169245" "basis" 1148 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-49309" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\pi +4"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "e64186c4f6d99028dc5d92ef4aab7f082fa435dfa296219d1ca4c5359c169245" "basis" 1148 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-49309" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\pi +8"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "e64186c4f6d99028dc5d92ef4aab7f082fa435dfa296219d1ca4c5359c169245" "basis" 1148 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-49309" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\pi +4"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "e64186c4f6d99028dc5d92ef4aab7f082fa435dfa296219d1ca4c5359c169245" "basis" 1148 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-49309" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\pi +8"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "e64186c4f6d99028dc5d92ef4aab7f082fa435dfa296219d1ca4c5359c169245" "basis" 1148 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-346576" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The total number of pages of a document printed $t$ minutes after printing begins increases at a rate of $\\frac{18}{(t+1)^{2}}.$ If, at $t=0$ minutes, the printer had already produced $40$ pages, how many pages will the printer have produced after $2$ minutes?\n\nAfter $2$ minutes the printer has produced\n{{field-1}} pages." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "e64186c4f6d99028dc5d92ef4aab7f082fa435dfa296219d1ca4c5359c169245" "basis" 1148 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-346576" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $a(t)=\\frac{18}{(t+1)^{2}}$ be the rate at which the number of pages produced is changing, and let $A(T)$ be the number of pages produced $T$ minutes after printing begins. \n\nUsing our formula for the accumulation of change, we have\n\n$$\nA(T)=A(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}a(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(T) & =A(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =40+{\\int }_{0}^{T}\\frac{18}{(t+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =40+(-\\frac{18}{t+1}){|}_{0}^{T} \\\\  & =40+(-\\frac{18}{T+1})-(-\\frac{18}{1}) \\\\  & =40-\\frac{18}{T+1}+18 \\\\  & =58-\\frac{18}{T+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the number of pages produced $T=2$ minutes after printing begins, we substitute in the above:\n\n$$\nA(2)=40-\\frac{18}{2+1}+18=52.\n$$\n\nThus, after $2$ minutes, the printer has produced $52$ pages." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "e64186c4f6d99028dc5d92ef4aab7f082fa435dfa296219d1ca4c5359c169245" "basis" 1148 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-346576" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "da3ddabd6e1b99db68538a49fbeacfe91dd1f81d24b1020a197b6e80597ac2ba" "basis" 1148 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-346576" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/state.json" "file_sha256" "e64186c4f6d99028dc5d92ef4aab7f082fa435dfa296219d1ca4c5359c169245" "basis" 1148 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-49308" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068772/assets/288b3eeaf1168316f2d8f43fa9484032daa8c9006a1c8355b8edfd1186dc2add.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Given $g(t)={\\int }_{-4}^{t}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ determine the value of $g(2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068772/state.json" "file_sha256" "abc79960513f9148d6a4a548b01029920dbd636ae2c830d2e4478ac6411bdd12"} {"question" "q-49308" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=2,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(2)={\\int }_{-4}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068772/assets/9f570d7f04bcf5645a97a6a1d85e72f602c436ef184e841fc4c998c0d5eb8e2e.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x\\in [-4,-1],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a rectangle. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{1}.$\n\n- $x\\in [-1,2],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a trapezoid. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(2)={\\int }_{-4}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x\\in [-4,-1],$ we compute the area ${A}_{1}$ of the rectangle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x\\in [-1,2],$ we compute the area ${A}_{2}$ of the trapezoid as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot (1+2) \\\\  & =4.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(2) & ={\\int }_{-4}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=6+4.5=10.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068772/state.json" "file_sha256" "abc79960513f9148d6a4a548b01029920dbd636ae2c830d2e4478ac6411bdd12"} {"question" "q-49308" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068772/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1c8d43860e1f2cb32aaf58a9138e6681583993041a63f478c9d32aa9104b6dba"} {"question" "q-49308" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068772/state.json" "file_sha256" "abc79960513f9148d6a4a548b01029920dbd636ae2c830d2e4478ac6411bdd12"} {"question" "q-49308" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10.5"] ["math" "11.5"] ["math" "15"] ["math" "9"] ["math" "9.5"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068772/state.json" "file_sha256" "abc79960513f9148d6a4a548b01029920dbd636ae2c830d2e4478ac6411bdd12"} {"question" "q-49308" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "10.5"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068772/state.json" "file_sha256" "abc79960513f9148d6a4a548b01029920dbd636ae2c830d2e4478ac6411bdd12"} {"question" "q-49309" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068772/assets/af84278943fcf2d9c4e3b99c115a4d82672fe50f2bfed8be7be84648985f80df.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. It consists of two lines and the arc of a semi-circle of radius $2.$ Given $g(t)={\\int }_{-3}^{t}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ determine the value of $g(3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068772/state.json" "file_sha256" "abc79960513f9148d6a4a548b01029920dbd636ae2c830d2e4478ac6411bdd12"} {"question" "q-49309" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=3,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(3)={\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068772/assets/4d1f408db7fcb99092f7e61afc4c0a25501d3349790e54983f481408c58f9951.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x\\in [-3,1],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a semi-circle and a rectangle below it. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{1}.$\n\n- $x\\in [1,3],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a trapezoid. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(3)={\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x\\in [-3,1],$ we compute the area ${A}_{1}$ of the semi-circle and the rectangle below it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2}+bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}+4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\pi +4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x\\in [1,3],$ we compute the area ${A}_{2}$ of the trapezoid as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}h(a+b) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot (1+3) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(3) & ={\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=(2\\pi +4)+4=2\\pi +8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068772/state.json" "file_sha256" "abc79960513f9148d6a4a548b01029920dbd636ae2c830d2e4478ac6411bdd12"} {"question" "q-49309" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068772/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1c8d43860e1f2cb32aaf58a9138e6681583993041a63f478c9d32aa9104b6dba"} {"question" "q-49309" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068772/state.json" "file_sha256" "abc79960513f9148d6a4a548b01029920dbd636ae2c830d2e4478ac6411bdd12"} {"question" "q-49309" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\pi +16"] ["math" "2\\pi +4"] ["math" "2\\pi +8"] ["math" "\\pi +4"] ["math" "\\pi +8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068772/state.json" "file_sha256" "abc79960513f9148d6a4a548b01029920dbd636ae2c830d2e4478ac6411bdd12"} {"question" "q-49309" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\pi +8"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068772/state.json" "file_sha256" "abc79960513f9148d6a4a548b01029920dbd636ae2c830d2e4478ac6411bdd12"} {"question" "q-103778" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A gas station pump dispenses gas into an empty gas tank at a rate of $\\frac{11}{6}t$ liters per second. Write an expression for $A(t),$ the total volume of gas in the tank at time $t,$ where $t$ is the time measured in second." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068772/state.json" "file_sha256" "abc79960513f9148d6a4a548b01029920dbd636ae2c830d2e4478ac6411bdd12"} {"question" "q-103778" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $f(t)=\\frac{11}{6}t$ be the rate at which the gas pump dispenses gas, and let $A(T)$ be the total number of liters in the car after $T$ seconds.\n\nUsing our formula for the accumulation of change, we have\n\n$$\nA(T)=A(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(T) & =A(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =0+{\\int }_{0}^{T}\\frac{11}{6}t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={(\\frac{11}{6}\\cdot \\frac{{t}^{2}}{2})|}_{0}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{11{T}^{2}}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's rewrite our final answer in terms of the original variable $t.$ We have\n\n$$\nA(t)=\\frac{11{t}^{2}}{12}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068772/state.json" "file_sha256" "abc79960513f9148d6a4a548b01029920dbd636ae2c830d2e4478ac6411bdd12"} {"question" "q-103778" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068772/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1c8d43860e1f2cb32aaf58a9138e6681583993041a63f478c9d32aa9104b6dba"} {"question" "q-103778" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068772/state.json" "file_sha256" "abc79960513f9148d6a4a548b01029920dbd636ae2c830d2e4478ac6411bdd12"} {"question" "q-103778" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "A(t)=5+\\frac{11{t}^{2}}{12}"] ["math" "A(t)=5+\\frac{5}{6}t"] ["math" "A(t)=5+\\frac{5}{6}t+\\frac{11{t}^{2}}{12}"] ["math" "A(t)=\\frac{11{t}^{2}}{12}"] ["math" "A(t)=\\frac{5}{6}t+\\frac{11{t}^{2}}{12}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068772/state.json" "file_sha256" "abc79960513f9148d6a4a548b01029920dbd636ae2c830d2e4478ac6411bdd12"} {"question" "q-103778" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "A(t)=\\frac{11{t}^{2}}{12}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068772/state.json" "file_sha256" "abc79960513f9148d6a4a548b01029920dbd636ae2c830d2e4478ac6411bdd12"} {"question" "q-346534" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate $F(9)$ where $F(x)={\\int }_{1}^{x}\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{t}}\\,\\text{d}t.$\n\n$F(9)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068772/state.json" "file_sha256" "abc79960513f9148d6a4a548b01029920dbd636ae2c830d2e4478ac6411bdd12"} {"question" "q-346534" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Evaluating $F(x)$ at $x=9,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\nF\\,(9)={\\int }_{1}^{9}\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{t}}\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nThen, computing the definite integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(9) & ={\\int }_{1}^{9}\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{t}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{1}^{9}{t}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =12{t}^{1/2}\\,{|}_{1}^{9} \\\\  & =12({9}^{1/2}-{1}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =12(3-1) \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068772/state.json" "file_sha256" "abc79960513f9148d6a4a548b01029920dbd636ae2c830d2e4478ac6411bdd12"} {"question" "q-346534" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068772/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1c8d43860e1f2cb32aaf58a9138e6681583993041a63f478c9d32aa9104b6dba"} {"question" "q-346534" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068772/state.json" "file_sha256" "abc79960513f9148d6a4a548b01029920dbd636ae2c830d2e4478ac6411bdd12"} {"question" "q-346534" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "24"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068772/state.json" "file_sha256" "abc79960513f9148d6a4a548b01029920dbd636ae2c830d2e4478ac6411bdd12"} {"question" "q-346576" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The total number of pages of a document printed $t$ minutes after printing begins increases at a rate of $\\frac{18}{(t+1)^{2}}.$ If, at $t=0$ minutes, the printer had already produced $40$ pages, how many pages will the printer have produced after $2$ minutes?\n\nAfter $2$ minutes the printer has produced\n{{field-1}} pages." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068772/state.json" "file_sha256" "abc79960513f9148d6a4a548b01029920dbd636ae2c830d2e4478ac6411bdd12"} {"question" "q-346576" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $a(t)=\\frac{18}{(t+1)^{2}}$ be the rate at which the number of pages produced is changing, and let $A(T)$ be the number of pages produced $T$ minutes after printing begins. \n\nUsing our formula for the accumulation of change, we have\n\n$$\nA(T)=A(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}a(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(T) & =A(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =40+{\\int }_{0}^{T}\\frac{18}{(t+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =40+(-\\frac{18}{t+1}){|}_{0}^{T} \\\\  & =40+(-\\frac{18}{T+1})-(-\\frac{18}{1}) \\\\  & =40-\\frac{18}{T+1}+18 \\\\  & =58-\\frac{18}{T+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the number of pages produced $T=2$ minutes after printing begins, we substitute in the above:\n\n$$\nA(2)=40-\\frac{18}{2+1}+18=52.\n$$\n\nThus, after $2$ minutes, the printer has produced $52$ pages." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068772/state.json" "file_sha256" "abc79960513f9148d6a4a548b01029920dbd636ae2c830d2e4478ac6411bdd12"} {"question" "q-346576" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068772/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1c8d43860e1f2cb32aaf58a9138e6681583993041a63f478c9d32aa9104b6dba"} {"question" "q-346576" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068772/state.json" "file_sha256" "abc79960513f9148d6a4a548b01029920dbd636ae2c830d2e4478ac6411bdd12"} {"question" "q-346576" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "52"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068772/state.json" "file_sha256" "abc79960513f9148d6a4a548b01029920dbd636ae2c830d2e4478ac6411bdd12"}] "usage" {"q-346576" [] "q-49309" []} "basis" 2320 "retractions" [[17592186289476 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/assets/af84278943fcf2d9c4e3b99c115a4d82672fe50f2bfed8be7be84648985f80df.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. It consists of two lines and the arc of a semi-circle of radius $2.$ Given $g(t)={\\int }_{-3}^{t}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ determine the value of $g(3).$"] [17592186289476 :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=3,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(3)={\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/assets/4d1f408db7fcb99092f7e61afc4c0a25501d3349790e54983f481408c58f9951.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x\\in [-3,1],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a semi-circle and a rectangle below it. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{1}.$\n\n- $x\\in [1,3],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a trapezoid. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(3)={\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x\\in [-3,1],$ we compute the area ${A}_{1}$ of the semi-circle and the rectangle below it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2}+bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}+4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\pi +4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x\\in [1,3],$ we compute the area ${A}_{2}$ of the trapezoid as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}h(a+b) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot (1+3) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(3) & ={\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=(2\\pi +4)+4=2\\pi +8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186289467 17592186289468 17592186289478 17592186289479 17592186289480 17592186289481 17592186289482] "immutable_fields" [[17592186289466 :answer-field/correct] [17592186289466 :answer-field/id] [17592186289466 :answer-field/key] [17592186289466 :answer-field/type] [17592186289477 :answer-field/correct] [17592186289477 :answer-field/id] [17592186289477 :answer-field/key] 